[Title "Outflanking with the King"]
[Goal "White to play and win"]
[Themes "king and pawn, opposition, outflanking"]
[Rating "1400"]
[Source "Elementary endgame technique"]
[FEN "3k4/8/3K4/3P4/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]

1. Kc6 {The king is already in front of its pawn on the sixth rank, which is what
wins the game. Black holds the opposition, so White sidesteps rather than pushes:
the king goes around, and the pawn waits.} Kc8
(1... Ke7 2. Kc7 {The same idea mirrored — the king takes the queening square
under control and the pawn simply runs.} Ke8 3. d6 Kf7 4. d7)
2. d6 {Only now, with the king escorting it.} Kd8 3. d7
{Not a check. Black's king is shouldered off both c8 and e8 by the white king and
the pawn between them.} Ke7 4. Kc7 {The pawn queens next move. The whole technique
was one sidestep at move one; pushing the pawn first would have thrown the win
away.}
